; =====================================================================
; cbor-seq-container.derived.cddl
;
; DERIVED - not verbatim. NONE of the four rules below is RFC 8742's.
; The document's own CDDL is cddl-fragments.cddl (verbatim, three
; fragments) and its dedented companion. This file is the artifact
; RFC 8742 section 4.1 says cannot be written, written anyway, four
; ways, with the cost of each stated - so that a DEC-002 ADR has
; something to choose between instead of a sentence of English.
;
; THE REASON IT IS DERIVED BY CONSTRUCTION. Section 4.1: "Currently,
; CDDL does not provide for unadorned CBOR Sequences as a top-level
; subject of a specification. For now, the suggestion is to use an array
; for the top-level rule, as is used for the ".cborseq" control
; operator, and add English text that explains that the specification is
; really about a CBOR Sequence with the elements of the array:"
; -- [cross-check] 2026-10-21, pass 3: this quotation used to close
; `array".`, dropping the source's terminal COLON (line 204 reads "the
; array:") and substituting a full stop outside the quote. The colon is
; load-bearing here: it is what introduces the F02 fragment the next
; sentence of §4.1 prints. ../design-notes.md §3.1 quotes the same two
; sentences WITH the colon, so the two artifacts disagreed about the
; same token, which hazards.md #3 says to treat as evidence rather than
; as a judgement call.
; cbor-seq.derived.abnf shows that ABNF cannot write it either. So under
; R-O-03 - an algorithm "SHALL be cited, not invented silently" - there
; is NOTHING TO CITE for a top-level sequence rule, and whatever we
; write is ours and must say so. That is what this file is for and why
; its name carries .derived.
;
; The dialect is CDDL as of RFC 8610 plus RFC 9682's corrected collected
; ABNF; no control operator from RFC 9741 or RFC 9165 is used. .cborseq
; and .cbor are RFC 8610 section 3.8.4's and are the document's own
; choices (rfc-8742#R-0029, #R-0038).
;
; Source of every quoted sentence: RFC 8742, cached source sha256
;   4462966836b60f42ebae9b5f1d2a479c328e16f0380a3434311a083bccbce30e
; =====================================================================

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; C1. THE SEQUENCE, AS SECTION 4.1 SAYS TO WRITE IT
;     An array rule that is not an array, corrected by a comment.
;     This is the document's own suggestion, instantiated for a log of
;     statements. The comment is NORMATIVE PROSE under section 4.1's
;     convention and has no machine meaning whatever.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

; This defines an array, the elements of which are to be used
; in a CBOR Sequence:
statement-log = [* statement]
statement     = #6.18(any) / any   ; stand-in; the real element type is D4's

; COST. The top-level rule asserts something false: a conforming
; instance of `statement-log` is a CBOR array, and the file is not one.
; No tool can check the file; every tool can check the lie. There is no
; count, no order binding and no terminator in the file - the array head
; that would carry the count exists only in the rule, never in the
; bytes. A validator handed the real file must first be told, in
; English, to wrap it.

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; C2. THE SEQUENCE WITH SECTION 4.3'S BYTE-STRING WRAPPER
;     The document's own mitigation (rfc-8742#R-0038, #R-0039), which it
;     offers for skipping and fan-out and which also buys bounded
;     recovery. Same top-level lie as C1.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

; This defines an array of CBOR byte strings, the elements of which
; are to be used in a CBOR Sequence:
statement-log-wrapped = [* wrapped-statement]
wrapped-statement     = bytes .cbor statement

; COST. One byte-string head per entry (1 byte for an entry under 24
; bytes, 2 under 256, 3 under 65536, 5 under 2**32). What it buys, in
; the document's words: an element can be skipped "without the need for
; decoding its substructure", elements can be "fanning out ... to
; multi-threaded decoding processes", and "within limits, this may also
; enable recovering from elements that internally are not well formed;
; the limitation is that the sequence of byte strings does need to be
; well formed as such." So the failure boundary moves in one level; it
; does not go away. This is tag 24's pattern WITH THE TAG REMOVED, which
; is the move rfc-8949's design notes already record for the signed
; payload - so C2 is the shape a tag-free profile converges on anyway.

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; C3. A DEFINITE-LENGTH ARRAY - THE ONE CONTAINER THAT IS BOTH TAG-FREE
;     AND EXPRESSIBLE. Section 2's own parenthetical (rfc-8742#R-0014).
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

statement-bundle = [* statement]   ; and the file IS this array

; COST, and it is the only honest one here: the count is in the bytes.
; A definite-length array head states n (1 byte for n < 24, 2 for
; n < 256, 3 for n < 65536, 5 for n < 2**32), is self-delimiting, and
; USES NO TAG - so it is exactly as admissible under a no-IANA-tags rule
; as the sequence is. What it gives up is the one thing the sequence
; buys: incremental append. You cannot append to a definite-length
; array without rewriting its head.
;
; NOTE THAT C1 AND C3 ARE THE SAME CDDL TEXT. That is the whole point:
; the rule is identical and only the FILE differs, by one array head.
; Under C1 the rule is a fiction the file does not satisfy; under C3 it
; is a fact the file does. examples/vectors.yaml D-0002 (010203) and
; D-0003 (83010203) are the two byte strings, three bytes against four
; for [1, 2, 3]; V-0005 and V-0004 are the §4.2 diagnostic notation
; they render.
;
; [cross-check] 2026-10-21, pass 3: those ids read "V-0004 and V-0005
; are the two byte strings", which is wrong twice over -- V-0004 and
; V-0005 are diagnostic-notation vectors with no hex, and the pairing
; was also inverted (V-0004 is [1, 2, 3], the FOUR-byte case). Decoded
; at pass 3 with an independent checker: D-0002 is 3 bytes and decodes
; to 3 items, D-0003 is 4 bytes and decodes to 1 array of three. Both
; ids were bare, so bin/check-id-joins never saw them.

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; C4. AN INDEFINITE-LENGTH ARRAY - OFFERED BY SECTION 2 AND EXCLUDED
;     HERE. Recorded so the exclusion is visible rather than silent.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

; statement-bundle-indefinite = [* statement]
;   encoded with major type 4, additional information 31, terminated by
;   the "break" stop code (RFC 8949 sections 3.2, 3.2.1).

; WHY IT IS COMMENTED OUT. CDDL cannot distinguish it from C3 - the same
; rule covers both encodings, which is rfc-8949's point that determinism
; is an ENCODER-side property the schema layer does not see. It would
; hand back the problem C3 solves, because an indefinite-length head
; carries no count. And every deterministic profile this library holds
; excludes it: RFC 8949 section 4.2.1 requires definite lengths under
; Core Deterministic Encoding, and draft-ietf-cbor-cde and
; draft-mcnally-deterministic-cbor both inherit that. It is in the
; source and it is not a candidate.

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHAT NONE OF THE FOUR SUPPLIES, and it is not a CDDL problem
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; A BINDING BETWEEN THE ELEMENTS. Section 5: "(COSE protections can be
; applied to an entire CBOR Sequence or to each of the elements of the
; sequence independently; in the latter case, additional effort may be
; required if there is a need to protect the relationship of the
; elements in the sequence.)" (rfc-8742#R-0042). C3's array head states
; n in the BYTES, so signing the array binds the count; C1 and C2 state
; n nowhere, so n signed statements are n facts and no fact about the
; bundle. No rule above can fix that, because it is a question about
; what the signature covers, not about what the schema admits. See
; ../design-notes.md, Cut 3.
; =====================================================================
